GUIDES2 = {
    "everyday-food": dict(
        slug="/en/everyday-cooking/",
        label="Everyday cooking",
        title="Everyday cooking: simple meals all week | Cibello",
        desc="Everyday cooking that fits the time you have and the food already in your kitchen. How to keep weeknight meals varied, and how Cibello helps from fridge to plate.",
        eyebrow="Everyday cooking without the daily decision",
        h1="Everyday cooking: simple meals that work all week",
        lead="Everyday cooking does not need to impress anyone. It needs to be doable at six o'clock on a Tuesday with what is in the fridge. This guide covers what makes weekday meals sustainable over months rather than days, and how Cibello helps you get from an open fridge door to a finished plate without scrolling through hundreds of recipes.",
        sections=[
            ("What makes everyday cooking work", """<p>Good everyday cooking has three qualities: it fits the time you actually have, it uses ingredients you already own or can pick up without a detour, and it can be varied enough that nobody groans when it appears again. It rarely means new recipes every night. Most households rotate between 10 and 15 dishes, and that is perfectly sensible. The difference between a stressful food week and a calm one is usually planning and overview, not cooking skill.</p><p>It also helps to lower the bar on purpose. A weeknight meal should fill people up, taste good enough to be eaten without complaint and leave as little washing-up as possible. Anything beyond that is a bonus you can save for the weekend.</p>"""),
            ("Recipes that start in the fridge, not the recipe book", """<p>Most recipe sites begin at the wrong end: you choose a dish, then discover that half the ingredients are missing. Cibello turns the order around. Once your fridge, freezer and cupboards are registered, by photographing them or scanning receipts, the app matches your food inventory against more than 9,000 recipes and shows what share of the ingredients you already have. Dishes with 80 to 100 percent at home rise to the top, so <a href="/en/recipes-with-ingredients/">recipes with ingredients you have</a> can be cooked tonight rather than pinned for later.</p><ul><li><strong>Pasta and noodles</strong>: there is nearly always something in the cupboard, and it pairs with whatever the fridge holds.</li><li><strong>Omelettes, frittatas and pancakes</strong>: rescue eggs, milk and vegetables that are close to their date.</li><li><strong>Stews, curries and soups</strong>: forgiving of substitutions and happy to become <a href="/en/meal-prep/">tomorrow's lunch</a>.</li><li><strong>Stir-fries and grain bowls</strong>: use up half a pepper, a lonely carrot and yesterday's rice.</li><li><strong>Traybakes</strong>: root vegetables, chicken or fish on one tray with very little effort.</li><li><strong>Jacket potatoes</strong>: a cupboard staple topped with whatever needs eating.</li></ul>"""),
            ("Planning the week without planning every minute", """<p>Plan five weekdays at once and each individual evening gets easier. A good <a href="/en/meal-planner/">weekly meal plan</a> takes into account when there is time to cook, what should be used first and which dishes leave leftovers for the next day. Cibello varies ingredients and types of meal so the same thing does not come round several days running, and gathers whatever is missing into a <a href="/en/shopping-list-app/">shared shopping list</a>. You review and change the plan before the week starts. The suggestions are help, not homework.</p><p>A simple pattern that suits many households: two quick favourites, one dish that takes a little longer, one leftovers night and one evening for clearing out the freezer. That leaves room for the unexpected, and nobody has to improvise at five o'clock.</p>"""),
            ("Quick, affordable and child-friendly, depending on the week", """<p>Some weeks need to be optimised for time, others for money or for small people who prefer the familiar. When the week is packed, <a href="/en/quick-dinner-ideas/">quick dinners in 20 to 30 minutes</a> are the right starting point. When the budget is tight, the answer is to cook from what is already at home and let the cupboard carry more of the week. And when children are at the table, <a href="/en/family-dinner-ideas/">family dinner ideas</a> that can be varied without becoming unfamiliar keep the peace.</p><p>Cibello starts from what is in your kitchen, your allergies and your food habits, and over time it learns what your household tends to choose. Each suggestion comes with a short explanation of why it appears, for instance because you have chicken and cream that will expire soon. Allergy and diet filters are guidance rather than a guarantee, so always check ingredients and packaging yourself.</p>"""),
            ("Leftovers, the freezer and lunch tomorrow", """<p>The easiest weeknight meal is the one that is already cooked. Double the quantity of stews, bolognese, soups and bakes and put half straight in the freezer. That gives you an evening next week when dinner only needs reheating. Leftovers can also be logged as meal boxes in Cibello's food inventory, so they stay part of the overview instead of being forgotten at the back of the fridge.</p><p>The app gives a gentle nudge about what should be used soon, with no finger-wagging. Combined with an up-to-date inventory, that means <a href="/en/reduce-food-waste/">less food waste</a> becomes a side effect of cooking dinner rather than a project in its own right.</p>"""),
            ("How to keep everyday cooking going for the long run", """<ol><li>Register what you have at home once, then update it when you shop. Receipts and barcodes can be scanned, and you review the result before it is saved, because AI can misread a label.</li><li>Let the suggestions start from the inventory rather than from a blank page.</li><li>Plan three to five dinners at a time, not seven. Leave air for leftovers and spontaneity.</li><li>Cook double portions of dishes that freeze well and stock the freezer with ready meals of your own making.</li><li>Always check ingredients, allergens and dates yourself. The app is a support, not a guarantee.</li></ol>"""),
        ],
        faq=[
            ("What counts as everyday cooking?", "Meals you can make on an ordinary weeknight: quick, based on ingredients you have or can easily get, and varied enough to repeat across the week without anyone tiring of them."),
            ("How do I find recipes for what I already have at home?", "In Cibello you register your fridge, freezer and cupboards by photographing them or scanning receipts. The app then matches your inventory against more than 9,000 recipes and shows what share of the ingredients you already have."),
            ("Can Cibello plan my weekday meals for the whole week?", "Yes. Cibello can suggest a weekly plan that varies dishes and ingredients and adds whatever is missing to a shared shopping list. You review and change the plan yourself."),
            ("Do the suggestions take allergies into account?", "You can set allergies and food habits, but the filtering is guidance and not a guarantee. Always check ingredients and packaging yourself."),
        ],
    ),

    "quick-dinner": dict(
        slug="/en/quick-dinner-ideas/",
        label="Quick dinners",
        title="Quick dinner ideas in 20 to 30 minutes | Cibello",
        desc="Quick dinner ideas you can cook in 20 to 30 minutes from what is already in the kitchen, a table of dishes that nearly always work, and how Cibello finds them for you.",
        eyebrow="Dinner in 20 to 30 minutes",
        h1="Quick dinner ideas: on the table in 20 to 30 minutes",
        lead="A quick dinner is only quick if you already have the ingredients. This guide collects dishes that nearly always work from a normal fridge and cupboard, shows how to keep them from all turning into pasta, and explains how Cibello finds a suggestion from what is actually in your kitchen.",
        sections=[
            ("Why quick dinners go wrong", """<p>Most recipes labelled quick assume you have everything to hand. In real life a couple of things are usually missing, and 20 minutes becomes 50 with a trip to the shop. The other common problem is that all the quick dishes end up the same: pasta, pasta and pasta again. The fix for both is to let the suggestions start from what you actually have, and to let something other than your memory keep track of the variety.</p><p>There is a third trap, too: quick dinners that need a long shopping list of small, specific items. A dish that takes 20 minutes but requires three fresh herbs and a jar you will use once is not a weeknight dish. The ones below rely on things you probably keep anyway.</p>"""),
            ("Quick dinners you can nearly always make", """<div class="table-wrap"><table class="cmp"><caption>Times assume the ingredients are already in the kitchen. Swap the protein or vegetables for whatever needs using.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Dish</th><th scope="col">Time</th><th scope="col">Built on</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Pasta with whatever is in the fridge</th><td>15–20 min</td><td>Pasta, cheese, vegetables, perhaps bacon or chickpeas</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Omelette or frittata</th><td>15 min</td><td>Eggs, leftover vegetables, cheese</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Stir-fry with rice or noodles</th><td>20 min</td><td>Half-used vegetables, any protein, soy sauce</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Tacos, wraps or quesadillas</th><td>20 min</td><td>Mince, beans or chicken, tortillas</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Soup from lentils or root vegetables</th><td>25–30 min</td><td>Cupboard staples plus what needs using</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Baked fish with vegetables</th><td>25 min</td><td>Frozen fish, potatoes, lemon</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Quick curry with chicken or chickpeas</th><td>25–30 min</td><td>Tinned tomatoes, coconut milk, spices</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Jacket potatoes with a topping</th><td>20 min</td><td>Potatoes started in the microwave, beans, cheese, tuna or leftovers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>What these dishes share is that they tolerate change. No chicken? Beans will do. No pepper? A carrot works. Cibello shows which ingredients you have and which are missing, so you can see in a moment whether a recipe works as written or needs adapting.</p>"""),
            ("A dinner suggestion in under a minute", """<ol><li>Photograph the fridge and cupboards. The app recognises the items and builds your food inventory. You check the result before saving it, since AI can misread a label.</li><li>Ask what to eat and browse suggestions that start from what is at home and what should be used first.</li><li>Pick from the dishes with the highest share of ingredients already in the kitchen.</li><li>Add anything missing to the shopping list, or choose something else.</li></ol><p>The suggestions also weigh in what is about to expire. A quick dinner that rescues the cream with two days left on it is doubly useful. Read more about <a href="/en/best-before-dates/">best before and use by dates</a> and about <a href="/en/reduce-food-waste/">reducing food waste</a> at home.</p>"""),
            ("Vary the protein, not the dish", """<p>The dishes in the table are frameworks rather than recipes. The same stir-fry works with chicken one week, prawns the next and tofu the week after. The same tacos take mince, fish or black beans. If you think in frameworks, you can keep a short list of dishes everyone likes and still eat something different every night. Cibello learns what your household tends to pick and suggests variations that resemble your favourites but use what is in the kitchen right now.</p>"""),
            ("Quick dinners with children at the table", """<p>With children, the quick dinner usually also needs to be recognisable. Tacos, pancakes, meatballs and pasta with tomato sauce work for a reason. The trick is to vary the sides and the protein rather than the dish itself, and to let children assemble their own plate where possible. More ideas are in our guide to <a href="/en/family-dinner-ideas/">family dinner ideas</a>, and if you would rather plan a little further ahead, the <a href="/en/meal-planner/">weekly meal planner</a> handles a week of quick weekday dishes at once.</p>"""),
            ("When you have no idea at all", """<p>Some evenings the question is not how quickly but what. The <a href="/en/what-to-eat-tonight/">dinner picker</a> on our what to eat tonight page offers a suggestion from forty everyday dishes with no account needed. In the app the suggestions go one step further and start from what is actually in your fridge, with an explanation of why each dish is proposed. Either way, the aim is the same: to make the decision take less time than the cooking.</p>"""),
        ],
        faq=[
            ("What counts as a quick dinner?", "A quick dinner can be cooked in 20 to 30 minutes from ingredients you already have, without a trip to the shop first."),
            ("How do I find a quick dinner from what I have at home?", "Register your fridge and cupboards in Cibello by photographing them. The app matches your inventory against recipes and shows dishes you can cook now, with what should be used first at the top."),
            ("Can I see what is missing from a recipe?", "Yes. Cibello shows which ingredients are missing from each recipe. Always check ingredients and allergens yourself."),
            ("Which quick dinners suit children?", "Tacos, pancakes, pasta with tomato sauce and meatballs tend to work. Vary the sides and the protein rather than the whole dish."),
        ],
    ),

    "family-dinner": dict(
        slug="/en/family-dinner-ideas/",
        label="Family dinners",
        title="Family dinner ideas children will eat | Cibello",
        desc="Family dinner ideas children accept and adults enjoy: dish families you can vary, a weekly menu that stops the nagging, and how Cibello helps the whole household.",
        eyebrow="Dinner for the whole family",
        h1="Family dinner ideas that children will actually eat",
        lead="Most children like what they know. That does not mean eating the same thing every week, only that variety has to happen inside dishes they recognise. Here are the dish families that work for many families, a weekly menu that takes the argument out of the evening, and how Cibello keeps everyone looking at the same plan.",
        sections=[
            ("Dishes that children accept and you can still vary", """<p>Think in families of dishes rather than individual recipes. A child who eats pasta with tomato sauce will often eat pasta with a different sauce next week, as long as the shape on the plate is familiar.</p><ul><li><strong>Pasta</strong>: tomato sauce, bolognese, chicken and cream, pesto, cheese and broccoli.</li><li><strong>Tacos, wraps and fajitas</strong>: mince, chicken, beans or fish, and the children build their own.</li><li><strong>Pancakes</strong>: with bacon and peas for dinner, or rolled around a savoury filling.</li><li><strong>Meatballs and burgers</strong>: beef, chicken or vegetarian, with potatoes, mash or pasta.</li><li><strong>Traybakes</strong>: sausages or chicken with root vegetables, everything on one tray.</li><li><strong>Soup with bread</strong>: tomato, carrot or chicken, blended smooth for the youngest.</li><li><strong>Jacket potatoes and rice bowls</strong>: a plain base with toppings everyone chooses for themselves.</li></ul>"""),
            ("A weekly menu for the family", """<p>A <a href="/en/meal-planner/">weekly meal plan</a> cuts the nagging in both directions: the children know what is coming, and you do not have to improvise at five o'clock. A pattern that works for many families is two quick favourites, one new dish, one leftovers night and a Friday dinner everyone looks forward to. In Cibello you place the dishes on the days you like, and whatever is missing goes into the <a href="/en/shopping-list-app/">shared shopping list</a>, so whoever shops on the way home sees the same list as the person who planned.</p><p>Put the new dish on an evening when there is time and patience for it, never on the night after swimming. Serve it alongside something safe, so trying it is optional and the meal still works if it is refused.</p>"""),
            ("Quick and affordable without giving up on nutrition", """<p>Families with children rarely have time to cook for long on weekdays, and food is a large part of the household budget. The foundation for both <a href="/en/quick-dinner-ideas/">quick dinners</a> and cooking on a budget is the same: use what is there, plan for leftovers and buy mainly what is missing. Cibello shows nutritional values as a guide when you plan, but they are estimates and not dietary advice. If a child has particular needs, talk to your health visitor, GP or paediatrician.</p><p>A practical example: a pan of bolognese on Monday becomes chilli on Wednesday with a tin of beans and a spoon of spice, and the rice cooked for Wednesday becomes egg fried rice on Thursday. Three dinners from one pan of mince, and nobody at the table notices they have been eating leftovers.</p>"""),
            ("Allergies and children", """<p>If a child has an allergy or intolerance, it can be entered in the household profile so that suggestions are filtered. That filtering is guidance and never a guarantee. Always read the ingredients list and check the packaging yourself, particularly when a recipe is adapted or a product is swapped for another brand. The same applies to recipes from any source, including this one. If in doubt, leave the ingredient out or choose a different dish rather than trusting a filter.</p>"""),
            ("Let the children take part", """<p>Children who get to choose between two dinners, or build their own taco, are more often positive at the table. The app is for adults aged 18 and over, but the choosing can happily happen together at the kitchen table: you show two suggestions, they pick one. Cibello is built for the whole household, so everyone with access sees the same food inventory, plan and shopping list, and the suggestions learn what you tend to like without anyone having to hold it all in their head.</p>"""),
            ("When the evening goes wrong anyway", """<p>Some nights nothing works: the planned dish is refused, someone is late and the fridge looks empty. A short fallback list helps here. Pasta with butter and cheese, scrambled eggs on toast, a tortilla pizza or beans on a jacket potato take ten minutes and are rarely rejected. Keep the ingredients for two of them in the house at all times, and let the app remind you when they are running short. A bad evening is then just a bad evening, not a reason to abandon the plan.</p>"""),
        ],
        faq=[
            ("Which dinners do children usually like?", "Pasta, tacos, pancakes, meatballs and traybakes work for many children. Vary the sauces, sides and protein rather than the whole dish."),
            ("How do I make a weekly menu for the family?", "Choose two quick favourites, one new dish, one leftovers night and a weekend dinner. In Cibello you place the dishes on the days you like and get whatever is missing in a shared shopping list."),
            ("Can Cibello take my children's allergies into account?", "You can enter allergies in the household profile, but the filtering is guidance. Always check ingredients and packaging yourself."),
            ("Are the nutritional values in the app suitable for children?", "Nutritional values are estimates for planning, not dietary advice. If your child has particular needs, talk to a health professional."),
        ],
    ),

    "meal-boxes": dict(
        slug="/en/meal-prep/",
        label="Meal prep",
        title="Meal prep: batch cooking and lunchbox ideas | Cibello",
        desc="Meal prep that works: dishes that survive the box, a plan for the week, fridge and freezer times, food safety basics, and how Cibello keeps track of what is in there.",
        eyebrow="Cook once, eat twice",
        h1="Meal prep: batch cooking, lunchboxes and leftovers that get eaten",
        lead="Meal prep does not have to mean a Sunday spent filling twenty identical containers. It can mean doubling tonight's dinner and knowing where the second half went. This guide covers which dishes survive the box, how to plan a week of it, how long things keep and how Cibello helps you eat what you prepared.",
        sections=[
            ("Dishes that survive the box", """<p>Good meal prep survives a day or two in the fridge and a few minutes in the microwave without becoming sad. Stews, curries, chilli, bolognese, lasagne, pasta bakes, grain salads with bulgur or rice and roasted root vegetables with a protein are safe bets. Anything with crunchy toppings, fresh salad or a fried egg is better packed separately and added when you eat. Cook a double batch of an ordinary weeknight dish and the lunchboxes make themselves. Flat, stackable containers with tight lids make the fridge easier to read and the freezer easier to fill. See <a href="/en/everyday-cooking/">everyday cooking</a> for dishes that suit doubling.</p><p>Rice and pasta are fine in a box but behave differently. Rice dries out and is better with a sauce over it; pasta softens, so cook it a minute short if it is destined for the fridge.</p>"""),
            ("Planning a week of meal prep", """<ol><li>Choose two dishes that keep well and cook double portions on Sunday or Monday.</li><li>Vary the sides: the same curry with rice one day and with flatbread and yoghurt the next.</li><li>Freeze half straight away. Chilled boxes should be eaten within a couple of days; frozen ones keep for months.</li><li>Log the boxes in Cibello's food inventory, so you know what is there and what should be eaten first.</li></ol><p>Enter the leftovers as meal boxes in Cibello and they sit in the inventory alongside everything else. When you plan the next few days, they are part of the picture instead of a surprise at the back of the shelf.</p>"""),
            ("Fridge or freezer", """<p>The fridge is for boxes you will eat within two or three days; the freezer is for everything else. Freezing stops the clock on safety, although texture slowly declines over months, so label boxes with contents and date or let the app hold that information. Defrost in the fridge overnight where you can, and reheat until piping hot all the way through. A box that has been defrosted should be eaten, not refrozen.</p><p>Portion before freezing rather than after. A single serving defrosts faster, goes straight from freezer to microwave on a busy evening and means you never have to thaw a brick of chilli to get at one lunch.</p>"""),
            ("Keeping track of what is in there", """<p>Most household food waste comes from things that were forgotten. A meal box at the back of the fridge is the classic case. In Cibello, meal boxes can be part of your food inventory, and the app reminds you gently when something should be eaten. Unsure how long something keeps? Read our guide to <a href="/en/best-before-dates/">best before and use by dates</a>, and always use smell, appearance and common sense as the final check.</p>"""),
            ("Safety and storage", """<ul><li>Cool food quickly after cooking and get it into the fridge within a couple of hours. Shallow containers cool faster than one deep pot.</li><li>Reheat boxes thoroughly, ideally so they are steaming throughout.</li><li>Cooked rice and pasta should be cooled quickly and eaten within a few days.</li><li>Label frozen boxes with contents and date, or let the app keep track.</li></ul><p>Dates and AI readings in the app are a support. Always check for yourself before eating, particularly when cooking for children, pregnant women or anyone with a weakened immune system.</p>"""),
            ("Meal prep without the Sunday marathon", """<p>The version of meal prep that lasts is the small one. Double one dish a couple of times a week, freeze half, and let the <a href="/en/meal-planner/">weekly meal plan</a> put those boxes to work on the evenings when there is no time to cook. Whatever is missing for the fresh dishes goes onto the <a href="/en/shopping-list-app/">shared shopping list</a>, and the boxes cover the rest. Less standing over pans on a Sunday, fewer takeaways on a Thursday, and <a href="/en/reduce-food-waste/">less food thrown away</a> along the way. If you only take one thing from this page, make it this: cook double once this week, freeze half and write down where it went.</p>"""),
        ],
        faq=[
            ("Which dishes work as meal prep?", "Stews, curries, chilli, bakes, grain salads with bulgur or rice and roasted root vegetables with a protein handle both the fridge and the microwave well."),
            ("How long does a meal box keep in the fridge?", "Count on a few days in the fridge and considerably longer in the freezer. Cool quickly, reheat thoroughly and always check smell and appearance."),
            ("Can Cibello keep track of my meal boxes?", "Yes. Meal boxes can be added to the food inventory, and the app reminds you when something should be eaten."),
            ("Can leftovers be part of the planning?", "Yes. Leftovers entered as meal boxes are part of the inventory and can be taken into account when the next few days are planned."),
        ],
    ),

    "shopping-list": dict(
        slug="/en/shopping-list-app/",
        label="Shopping list",
        title="Shared shopping list built from your recipes | Cibello",
        desc="A shopping list that only contains what is missing, fills itself from your meal plan and is shared with the household. How the shopping list in Cibello works, step by step.",
        eyebrow="Only what is missing",
        h1="A shared shopping list that starts from what you already have",
        lead="Most shopping lists are written from memory, by one person, and end up either too long or too short. Cibello's list starts from your food inventory and your planned meals, adds only what is missing and is shared with everyone in the household. Here is how it works and why it saves both money and food.",
        sections=[
            ("The trouble with ordinary shopping lists", """<p>A list written from memory tends to fail in one of two ways. Either it is too long, because you were not sure whether there was still rice at home, so you bought more. Or it is too short, because the one thing the recipe needed never made it onto the list. Shopping list apps solve the sharing problem, but not the connection to what is actually in the kitchen or what is going to be cooked. Someone still has to hold everything in their head, and that someone is usually the same person every week.</p>"""),
            ("How the shopping list in Cibello works", """<ol><li><strong>It starts from the inventory.</strong> When you plan a dish, its ingredients are compared with what is already in the fridge, freezer and cupboards. Only the missing items are added.</li><li><strong>It fills from the meal plan.</strong> Plan the week's dinners and all the gaps are collected in one list.</li><li><strong>It is shared with the household.</strong> Everyone sees the same list and can tick things off in the shop.</li><li><strong>It updates the inventory.</strong> Photograph the receipt after shopping and the items are registered back into your inventory.</li></ol><p>You can also add things by hand, of course: washing-up liquid, coffee or the items that should always be in the house.</p>"""),
            ("One list for the whole household", """<p>Cibello's household feature exists so that several people can work from the same food inventory, the same <a href="/en/meal-planner/">weekly meal plan</a> and the same shopping list. Whoever is on the way home sees what is missing, and whoever did the planning does not have to send the list separately. During the free trial, and afterwards with a paid plan, the household shares one overview. The trial lasts 14 days and does not require a card.</p><p>Anyone in the household can add to the list, so the person who notices the coffee is running low is the one who writes it down, at the moment they notice. The list is the same on every phone, and an item ticked off in the shop disappears for everyone.</p>"""),
            ("A list that cuts waste and cost", """<p>When the list only contains what is missing, most duplicate purchases disappear. When dishes that use up short-dated food are planned first, waste drops too. It is the same principle as in our guide to <a href="/en/reduce-food-waste/">reducing food waste</a>: overview before shopping. The saving is not dramatic on any single trip, but a jar of pesto you did not need to buy and a bag of spinach you actually ate add up over a year.</p><p>It also changes how you shop. A list you trust is one you follow, and that is where the impulse purchases and the third bag of salad tend to disappear. Shopping becomes a shorter, calmer errand rather than a walk round every aisle in case something is needed.</p>"""),
            ("After the shop: closing the loop", """<p>A shopping list is only half the system. The other half is what happens when the bags come home. In Cibello you can photograph the receipt or scan barcodes so the new items land in the inventory, ready to be matched against recipes for the rest of the week. You check the result before saving, because AI can misread a receipt line, and quantities and dates are yours to correct. From then on the <a href="/en/pantry-app/">pantry</a> knows what arrived, and tonight's suggestions can use it.</p>"""),
            ("A quiet week, in practice", """<p>Sunday: you review the suggested plan for the week and swap one dish. The list fills itself with what is missing. Monday: someone shops on the way home, ticking items off on their phone. The receipt is photographed at the kitchen table. Wednesday: the app notes that the chicken should be used soon and suggests it for Thursday. Friday: nothing is thrown away, and nobody has stood in the supermarket wondering whether there is still rice at home.</p>"""),
        ],
        faq=[
            ("Can the whole household see the same shopping list?", "Yes. The household feature in Cibello shares the food inventory, meal plan and shopping list between members, during the trial and afterwards with a paid plan."),
            ("Does the shopping list fill itself automatically?", "Yes. When you plan recipes or a weekly menu, the ingredients missing from your inventory are added to the list."),
            ("Can I add my own items to the list?", "Yes, anything can be added by hand, including items that are not food."),
            ("Is the inventory updated after I shop?", "You can photograph the receipt or scan barcodes so the items are added to your inventory. Check the result, since AI can misread."),
        ],
    ),

    "best-before": dict(
        slug="/en/best-before-dates/",
        label="Best before dates",
        title="Best before vs use by: what the dates mean | Cibello",
        desc="Best before is about quality, use by is about safety. What the two dates mean, how long common foods keep after the date, and how Cibello helps you use food in time.",
        eyebrow="Two dates, two meanings",
        h1="Best before and use by: what the dates on food really mean",
        lead="Two short phrases on a packet cause a surprising amount of food to be thrown away. Best before is a quality mark; use by is a safety limit. Knowing the difference, and having a way to remember what is due when, saves food and money without taking any risk. Here is what the dates mean and how to keep track of them.",
        sections=[
            ("Best before: a quality date", """<p><strong>Best before</strong> states how long the producer guarantees full quality: taste, texture and nutritional value. After the date, the food is often still perfectly good to eat, provided it has been stored properly and the packaging is unopened. That applies to milk, yoghurt, eggs, hard cheese, tinned goods, dry staples and frozen food. Look, smell and taste: national food safety authorities across Europe describe that as a sensible way to judge food past its best before date. Across the EU, and in the UK, the two phrases are set by food information law, so the wording means the same thing wherever you shop.</p>"""),
            ("Use by: a safety date", """<p><strong>Use by</strong> appears on perishable foods where bacteria can multiply without the food smelling or looking wrong, for instance fresh chicken, mince, fresh fish and some ready meals. After that date the food should not be eaten, even if it seems fine. The safe option is to cook or freeze it on or before the date. Freezing stops the clock, and a frozen item can be cooked after defrosting. Once a pack is opened, follow the storage instructions on it, which usually shorten the time you have regardless of the printed date.</p>"""),
            ("How long do common foods keep?", """<div class="table-wrap"><table class="cmp"><caption>Guidance only. Follow the instructions on the pack and your own senses, and take extra care with food for children, pregnant women and anyone with a weakened immune system.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Food</th><th scope="col">Guide after best before (unopened, stored correctly)</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Milk</th><td>A few days up to a week</td><td>Smell and taste; sour milk is obvious</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Yoghurt and soft cheese</th><td>Often one to several weeks</td><td>The acidity protects; check for mould</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Eggs</th><td>Several weeks</td><td>An egg that floats in water should be thrown away</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Hard cheese</th><td>Weeks to months</td><td>Cut away surface mould on hard cheese</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Tinned and dry goods</th><td>Months to years</td><td>Discard dented, bulging or rusty tins</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Frozen food</th><td>Months</td><td>Quality declines, safety remains</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Cooked mince or chicken</th><td>2–3 days in the fridge</td><td>Cooked food: cool quickly, reheat thoroughly</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Cooked rice and pasta</th><td>A few days in the fridge</td><td>Cool quickly after cooking</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The table is guidance, not a rule. Follow the instructions on the packaging and trust your own senses, and be more cautious with food for children, pregnant women and people with a weakened immune system.</p>"""),
            ("Storing food so the dates hold", """<p>Dates assume correct storage. A fridge at 5 degrees or below, milk and meat on the lower shelves rather than in the door, and opened jars covered and chilled all extend the useful life of what you buy. Bread, onions and potatoes prefer a cool cupboard to the fridge. Eggs keep longer in the fridge than at room temperature. None of this is complicated; the hard part is remembering what is in there and when it went in.</p>"""),
            ("Why the confusion costs so much", """<p>A large share of the food thrown away at home was still fine to eat: yoghurt binned on the best before date, eggs discarded a week after it, bread that could have been frozen. Treating best before as a hard stop is an easy habit to fall into when you are tired and the date is printed in bold. Knowing that it is a quality date, and checking the food rather than the number, is a change that saves a great deal of food on its own. The second change is having a system that tells you what to use first, so the check happens before the food turns rather than after.</p>"""),
            ("Keeping track of the dates", """<p>The difficulty is not the rules but the memory. In Cibello, the items in your food inventory carry their dates, taken from receipts, barcodes or what you photographed, and the app gently flags what should be used soon. Suggestions for <a href="/en/what-to-eat-tonight/">tonight's dinner</a> then favour recipes that use exactly those items, so <a href="/en/reduce-food-waste/">less food waste</a> becomes a by-product of cooking dinner. Dates can be misread by AI, so check them yourself when you review a scan. <a href="/en/meal-prep/">Leftovers and meal boxes</a> can be added to the inventory too, so they are not forgotten.</p>"""),
        ],
        faq=[
            ("What is the difference between best before and use by?", "Best before is a quality date: the food is often fine to eat afterwards if it has been stored properly. Use by is a safety date for perishable foods such as chicken and mince, and should not be passed."),
            ("Can you eat food after the best before date?", "Often, yes. Look, smell and taste. This does not apply to food marked with a use by date."),
            ("How long does milk keep after the best before date?", "Often a few days up to a week if it has stayed unopened and cold. Smell and taste decide."),
            ("Can an app remind me before food goes off?", "Yes. Cibello keeps dates in your food inventory, reminds you when something should be used and suggests recipes that use it. Always check the dates yourself."),
        ],
    ),

    "weekday-dinners": dict(
        slug="/en/weeknight-dinner-ideas/",
        label="Weeknight dinners",
        title="25 weeknight dinner ideas sorted by time | Cibello",
        desc="Twenty-five weeknight dinner ideas sorted by how long they take, from 15-minute plates to 30-minute stews, plus the trick that makes them possible: letting the fridge choose.",
        eyebrow="Weeknight dinners, sorted by time",
        h1="25 weeknight dinner ideas, sorted by how long they take",
        lead="On a weeknight the question is rarely what would be nice but what can be done in the time there is. These 25 dinners are sorted by minutes, use ingredients most kitchens hold, and can be varied by swapping the protein. At the end: how Cibello makes sure the ingredients are actually there.",
        sections=[
            ("Ready in 15 minutes", """<p>These are for the evenings when the cooking has to be shorter than the commute. They lean on the cupboard and on whatever is left in the fridge, and none of them needs a recipe once you have made them twice.</p><ul><li><strong>Tuna pasta</strong>: pasta, tinned tuna, crème fraîche or a spoon of mayonnaise, lemon.</li><li><strong>Egg fried rice</strong>: yesterday's rice, eggs, frozen vegetables, soy sauce.</li><li><strong>Tortilla pizzas</strong>: tortillas, passata, cheese and whatever needs using.</li><li><strong>Pesto pasta with chicken</strong>: leftover roast chicken or a ready-cooked breast.</li><li><strong>Omelette with cheese and vegetables</strong>: rescues eggs and half-used vegetables.</li><li><strong>Halloumi wraps</strong>: halloumi, tortillas, salad, yoghurt dressing.</li><li><strong>Beans on toast, upgraded</strong>: good bread, beans, grated cheese, a fried egg on top.</li></ul>"""),
            ("Ready in 20 to 25 minutes", """<p>A little more time buys a hob and an oven working together, or a sauce that has had a few minutes to come together. Most of these feed four from a normal weekly shop.</p><ul><li><strong>Sausage stroganoff with rice</strong>.</li><li><strong>Chicken stir-fry with noodles</strong>.</li><li><strong>Tacos</strong> with mince, chicken or beans.</li><li><strong>Spaghetti carbonara</strong>.</li><li><strong>Baked salmon with potatoes and lemon</strong>.</li><li><strong>Falafel in pitta with salad</strong>.</li><li><strong>Tomato soup with grilled cheese sandwiches</strong>.</li><li><strong>Chickpea and spinach curry</strong>.</li><li><strong>Bubble and squeak from leftovers</strong>, with a fried egg.</li><li><strong>Salmon poke bowl with rice and avocado</strong>.</li></ul>"""),
            ("Ready in 30 minutes", """<p>Half an hour is enough for a stew or a bake, and most of these double happily for <a href="/en/meal-prep/">lunchboxes</a> later in the week. Cook one of them on a quieter evening and the busiest night of the week is already solved.</p><ul><li><strong>Creamy chicken and mushroom casserole</strong>.</li><li><strong>Lentil soup with bread</strong>.</li><li><strong>Vegetable chilli</strong>.</li><li><strong>Chicken curry with coconut milk</strong>: cook double for the freezer.</li><li><strong>Meatballs with mash</strong>.</li><li><strong>Sausage and root vegetable traybake</strong>.</li><li><strong>Pasta bake with broccoli and cheese</strong>.</li><li><strong>Bean burgers with potato wedges</strong>.</li></ul><p>More quick dishes, with a table of times and ingredients, are in the guide to <a href="/en/quick-dinner-ideas/">quick dinner ideas</a>. With children at the table: <a href="/en/family-dinner-ideas/">family dinner ideas</a>.</p>"""),
            ("The trick: let the fridge choose", """<p>Every dish above assumes the ingredients are actually in the house. That is where weeknight dinner usually falls apart: you choose the dish first and then discover the peppers are gone. Cibello turns the order around. Photograph the fridge and cupboards, and the app knows what is there; <a href="/en/recipes-with-ingredients/">recipes with ingredients you have</a> are ranked by how much of each dish is already in your kitchen, with items close to their date at the top. Four weekdays at a time become a <a href="/en/meal-planner/">weekly meal plan</a>, and whatever is missing lands in a shared shopping list. You review each scan before it is saved, because AI can misread a label.</p>"""),
            ("Rotation beats inspiration", """<p>Most households eat 10 to 15 dishes on rotation, and there is nothing wrong with that. The trick for variety is to swap the protein and the sides rather than the whole dish: the same casserole with chicken one week and chickpeas the next, the same tacos with fish instead of mince. Cibello learns what you tend to choose and suggests variations that resemble your favourites but use what is in the kitchen right now. <a href="/en/everyday-cooking/">Read more about everyday cooking that lasts the whole week</a>.</p>"""),
            ("Make Monday easier on Sunday", """<p>Ten minutes on Sunday evening is enough to place three or four of the dishes above on the days when they fit, and to check that the ingredients for the 15-minute options are in the house. That is the whole system. If the week changes, the plan changes with it, and any dish that did not get cooked simply moves. Fewer decisions at six o'clock, less food forgotten, and the same rotation of dishes everyone already likes.</p><p>If Sunday evening is not realistic, any ten minutes will do: on the bus, in a waiting room, or while the kettle boils on Monday morning. The point is not the day but the habit of deciding once for several evenings instead of once every evening.</p>"""),
        ],
        faq=[
            ("What are good weeknight dinner ideas?", "Dishes that take 15 to 30 minutes and use items most kitchens hold: pasta, omelettes, stir-fries, tacos, soups, casseroles and traybakes. The list above has 25 of them."),
            ("How do I find dinner ideas based on what I have at home?", "Photograph your fridge and cupboards in Cibello. The app ranks more than 9,000 recipes by how much of each dish is already in your kitchen."),
            ("How do I get variety into weeknight meals?", "Swap the protein and the sides rather than the whole dish, plan three to four dinners at a time and include a leftovers night."),
            ("Can Cibello plan the whole week?", "Yes. The weekly meal plan varies dishes and ingredients and adds whatever is missing to a shared shopping list. You review and adjust the plan yourself."),
        ],
    ),
}

